    {
      "id": "usgs-field-measurements-ingest",
      "classification": "internal",
      "status": "active",
      "requires_terminal": false,
      "rationale": "Scheduled producer, not an operator workflow. Invoked by the daily/weekly/all cron cadences in .github/workflows/refresh.yml via scripts/refresh.py, never by a person, so no normal user path requires a terminal. It complements scripts/ingest_usgs_groundwater.py, which reads the NWIS Daily Values service and therefore keeps only the 36 PR wells carrying a continuous series; the OGC API field-measurements collection (api.waterdata.usgs.gov/ogcapi/v0, keyless, the successor to the decommissioned waterservices.usgs.gov/nwis/gwlevels/) adds the discrete-measurement wells that service structurally cannot see -- 82 wells and 4,392 measurements over ten years, against 36 wells today. The wells are rendered as utility_asset rows on the existing assets and map views. The groundwater_level readings reach the PRII hub through the canonical federation export (scripts/federation_export.py globs data/*_readings.jsonl), which per docs/adr/0001 is this producer's supported product surface -- this repo's own dashboard is diagnostic-only. They are deliberately NOT wired into scripts/build_alerts.py, which names its three reading files explicitly and does not glob: parameter 62610 (level above NGVD29) has the opposite concerning direction from 72019 (depth below land surface), and src/aguayluz/water_alerts.py drives _AQUIFER_METRICS with direction='high', so routing this file into that proxy would invert the aquifer-drawdown signal. They are also not selectable on the diagnostic dashboard: server/backend/app.py's READING_VECTOR_REGISTRY has no field-measurements vector, and monitoring_quality.py's SERIES_METADATA_REGISTRY declares groundwater_level with parameter_codes=['72019'] only. Both are tracked follow-ups, not claims made here.",
      "analysis": {
        "files": [
          "scripts/ingest_usgs_field_measurements.py"
        ],
        "symbols": [
          "scripts/ingest_usgs_field_measurements.py:asset_id_for",
